FLAGS WITH DOCKER RUN: 
docker run: Core Flags for Creating Containers
These are the foundational options for running a new container. They control its lifecycle, interaction, and core settings.
-d, --detach: Runs the container in the background. This frees up your terminal, and you can later use docker logs to see its output or docker attach to reconnect.
 * docker run -d nginx #-d flags means run nginx in the background, without it, this will take up the entire terminal
-it ( -i & -t combined): This is the most common combination for interactive work.
   -i, --interactive: Keeps the standard input (STDIN) open, even if you're not attached to the container.
   -t, --tty: Allocates a pseudo-TTY, giving you a proper terminal session. The combination -it is required to run an interactive shell like bash.
 * docker run -it iwemida bash
  # iwemida — The image to use. If it isn't already on your computer, Docker will automatically download the latest Ubuntu image from Docker Hub.
  # bash — The language to run inside the container. # can be sh
--name: Assigns a custom name to the container, which makes it easier to reference later instead of using the auto-generated ID.
--rm: Automatically removes the container when it exits. This is perfect for short-lived, temporary containers that you don't need to keep around,
 helping to prevent clutter.
 * docker container prune #removes all stopped container 
 * docker container rm 3d # imagine container's ID is 3d4bab29dd67, using the first few characters of an ID
 * docker container rm id1 id2 id3 #multiple argument
 * docker run -d --rm -it --name iwemida ubuntu sh -c 'while true; do date; sleep 1; done'
   # --rm - Automatically deletes the container after it stops.
  (Ctrl + P, then Ctrl + Q) - This safely detaches your terminal screen and drops you back onto your host machine's command line,
  leaving the container ticking away silently.
 * docker rm iwemida # command deletes the container entirely. It removes the container's filesystem, its logs, its network settings, and its metadata from your Docker host.
  # rm can't remove a working container except force delete:
 * docker rm -f iwemida or docker rm --force iwemida
 FOR CONTEXT: *docker kill iwemida && docker rm iwemida is equivalent to *docker rm --force iwemida 
   # This is because when kill is run, it stops but keeps the container & can be restarted with: *docker start iwemida.
   -p, --publish: Publishes a port from the container to the host. The format is host-port:container-port, allowing external traffic to reach a service inside the container.
  * docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx #Starts Nginx in the background and makes it accessible at http://localhost:8080.

docker run: Flags for Resource and Security Management
These flags give you control over how much system resources a container can use and the level of permissions it has.
-m, --memory: Sets a memory limit for the container. This is a critical flag to prevent a single container from consuming all the host's memory.
--cpus: Specifies the number of CPUs the container can use, such as --cpus="1.5".
--privileged: Grants extended privileges to the container, essentially giving it access to the host's devices and capabilities.
 Use this flag with caution, as it carries significant security risks.
--security-opt: Allows setting advanced security options, such as disabling AppArmor or Seccomp profiles.

docker run: Flags for Environment and Networking
These flags configure the container's environment and how it connects to networks.
-e, --env: Sets an environment variable inside the container, like -e MY_VAR=my_value.
-v, --volume: Mounts a volume or directory from the host into the container. The format is host-path:container-path. 
 This allows data to persist and be shared between the host and the container.
--network: Connects the container to a network. For example, --network host makes the container use the host's network stack.
--add-host: Adds a custom entry to the container's /etc/hosts file, allowing you to define hostname-to-IP mappings.

* docker run -it -d --name iwemida ubuntu sh -c ' while true; do date; sleep 3; done'
 # --name iwemida _ this overrides the feature that assigns random names and forces "iwemida"
 # ubuntu - this is the image ran to form a container, It's a lightweight, stripped-down version of the standard Ubuntu Linux OS
 # sh -c '...' _ By default, an Ubuntu container launches into a plain shell or does nothing and exits. This parameter tells Docker: 
   "Don't run the default command. Instead, open a standard Linux shell (sh) and execute the following string of commands (-c)."
 #'while true; do date; sleep 1; done' _This is a classic Linux infinite bash loop broken down into four parts:
    -while true; – Start a loop that runs forever because the condition "true" is always true.
    -do date; – Print the current system date and time to the standard output.
    -sleep 1; – Pause the script execution for exactly 1 second.
    done – Marks the end of the loop iteration, telling it to go back to the top and repeat.
